WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:03.800
- [Interviewer] All right, Tanya.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:04.700
Tanya, where are you from?

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.440
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.700
- [Tanya] L.A.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.893
- [Interviewer] South central?

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:10.733
- [Tanya] Yeah.

00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:11.637
- [Interviewer] And tell
me about your your family,

00:00:11.637 --> 00:00:13.487
you had both your parents growing up?

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.540
- [Tanya] Not really.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.100
Dad when I was, my
father hasn't really been

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.270
in my life since, about six.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.410
My mom died when I was very young.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:25.310
- [Interviewer] Your mom died?

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.143
- Yeah.

00:00:26.143 --> 00:00:27.250
- [Interviewer] I'm sorry.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.550
Where was dad?

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.520
Just out of the picture?

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.681
- Yeah, he was battling addiction.

00:00:34.681 --> 00:00:35.514
- [Interviewer] Oh I see.

00:00:35.514 --> 00:00:36.707
- So.

00:00:36.707 --> 00:00:40.390
- [Interviewer] And who raised you then?

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.343
- The village.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.520
- [Interviewer] So did you
go to the foster system or?

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.353
- No.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:47.560
- [Interviewer] When you say the village?

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.580
- Everybody, it takes a
village to raise a child.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.060
The neighbors, people you know,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.450
people you meet in your life.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:54.470
- [Interviewer] So not even family,

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.390
just neighbors and friends?

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.663
- Couple of family.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.210
Like an aunt or two.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.640
- [Interviewer] Were you bounced around?

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.873
- Yeah, I was all over.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.253
Young, hurt, parents gone.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:09.953
Super young.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.582
- [Interviewer] What kind of
kid were you in high school?

00:01:15.582 --> 00:01:16.499
- Good kid.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.233
Cheerleader.

00:01:20.595 --> 00:01:21.693
The ROTC.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.293
- [Interviewer] Was there any abuse

00:01:24.293 --> 00:01:25.954
when you were growing up?

00:01:25.954 --> 00:01:26.787
- No.

00:01:26.787 --> 00:01:28.900
- [Interviewer] No?

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.830
Then you went out on your own at what age?

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:31.663
- 13.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.190
- [Interviewer] You went to the street?

00:01:37.851 --> 00:01:39.766
When you worked Figueroa?

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:43.310
When did you start working the street?

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.683
- Right after like my mom passed.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.660
Like, I was lost.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.793
Plus, you know my aunt
did the best she could do.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.601
People, you know.

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:56.250
But I was always raised like, you know,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.683
you got to get your own.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.410
I see examples in front of me.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.590
You know, nobody wants to
take care of nobody else.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.623
Especially if it's not their own, so.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.430
I had to live, I had to eat, you know.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:10.630
- [Interviewer] What age did you start

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.101
working as a prostitute?

00:02:12.101 --> 00:02:13.128
- About 14.

00:02:13.128 --> 00:02:14.711
- [Interviewer] 14?

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.883
You worked on your own or with the pimp?

00:02:19.576 --> 00:02:24.576
- I've had people to look out
for me, but mainly on my own.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.060
Cause I was just one of those.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.620
No, I don't understand
giving you all my money.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.650
No, no.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.010
I'm trying to live and take
care of me and find my way.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.910
I'm not giving you my money.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:36.743
For what?

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.480
For you just to watch me?

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.010
I can watch myself.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.820
I'm doing the work.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.420
I'm going to keep it for myself.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:47.940
- [Interviewer] Have you
been arrested for this?

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:48.773
- Mm hmm.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:52.413
- [Interviewer] How many times?

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.540
- 10, 15?

00:02:58.540 --> 00:02:59.373
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.200
Did you ever had any bad experiences

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.823
with the guys that pick you up?

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.983
- Um, yeah. Rob.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:15.603
Picked up and dropped off
somewhere way out the district.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:18.790
By the grace of God,

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.930
I've never been seriously injured

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:22.870
or raped or nothing like that,

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.670
but I know a lot of people who have.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.200
And it's a scary world, scary life.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.937
I wouldn't recommend
anybody to go that way.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.053
- [Interviewer] So do you have any family

00:03:39.053 --> 00:03:40.370
to stay in contact with now?

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.143
- Some.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:44.910
I do. But...

00:03:49.368 --> 00:03:52.540
I really distanced myself
from a lot of people.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:53.643
My mom died.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.920
It did a lot to me. Caused
a lot emotional pain.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.220
Because I was a child
and I didn't understand.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.440
So, you know, I just did my own thing.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.940
Did my own thing.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.210
- [Interviewer] Are drugs a
part of the picture for you?

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:16.573
- I have tampered with, you know,

00:04:16.573 --> 00:04:17.880
a little bit here, a little bit there.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.090
But drinking, mainly.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:20.293
- [Interviewer] Drinking mostly?

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.690
Does this lifestyle
get you down sometimes?

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:25.710
- Of course it would.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:30.710
It's like, you're selling your
body on the street to man.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.450
Not because sometimes you want to do it.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.440
Cause like, well I know
I can get the money

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.273
for this for that.

00:04:38.273 --> 00:04:41.140
So that's what I'm gonna
do, but is it right?

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:42.513
Should you be? No.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:44.910
You shouldn't.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.690
And it's definitely, I should
have had somebody older

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.223
to guide me and support
me in the direction,

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.893
instead of letting me be freewill.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.750
- [Interviewer] Yeah. You'd
like that guidance as a kid.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:04:59.583
- Oh yeah.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.073
- [Interviewer] Did you have
friends doing the same thing?

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.630
- [Tanya] Maybe one or
two, but not really.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.180
They all still was kids at home

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.350
with their parents getting
punishments and all that shit.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:15.340
Sneaking out, running away.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.260
I never had a chance to like sneak out

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:21.100
or run away get caught before
the street lights came on.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.630
Like (beep), oh my God,
it's just past curfew.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.420
Damn, I stayed out at the party too long.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:26.253
I never. I did what I wanted to.

00:05:26.253 --> 00:05:28.010
- [Interviewer] You can stay up all night.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:29.003
- [Tanya] I did what I wanted to.

00:05:29.003 --> 00:05:30.690
I clubbed.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:31.633
I did everything.

00:05:32.557 --> 00:05:33.920
- [Interviewer] Do you
have kids of your own?

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.340
- [Tanya] I have a son.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.745
- [Interviewer] Are you raising him?

00:05:36.745 --> 00:05:38.523
- [Tanya] Not at this second.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:40.253
I was.

00:05:41.595 --> 00:05:42.700
But, things happen in life.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.570
There's some things
just out of our control.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:46.767
Not saying I'm a bad parent.

00:05:46.767 --> 00:05:48.780
Just life gets hard.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:52.563
Sometimes you may get a
curve ball thrown at you.

00:05:52.563 --> 00:05:54.430
- [Interviewer] Who's raising him now?

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:55.280
- [Tanya] Family.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.773
- [Interviewer] What was
the best time of your life?

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:02.780
- [Tanya] I have a lot of best times.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.230
You know, childhood before
my mom passed was great.

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:07.063
My son.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.063
Great.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.790
Understanding that
everything isn't your fault,

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:18.223
put a smile on my face.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.800
That you don't have to feel
embarrassed or shameful

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.753
for what you're going through,
what you had to go through.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.700
Because I mean, life ain't easy,

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.050
but I made it matter.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.480
I'm trying to make it better.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:36.510
That's what I know.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:39.110
As long as you're trying
to work towards better

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:41.653
that's what counts.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.690
But it's not going to be easy.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.470
You're going to go through a lot.

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:46.880
Like they say, you gotta go through a lot

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.873
of pain and hurt to get to the happy days.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.283
I mean, I can't cry over spilled milk.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:58.363
- [Interviewer] What was
the worst time of your life?

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:00.800
- Being on the streets.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.743
Sleepy. Tired.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:09.460
My mom dying.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:10.293
- [Interviewer] Your mom dying.

00:07:10.293 --> 00:07:11.126
- Losing my son.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:16.883
- [Interviewer] Have
you been in love before?

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:21.403
- Yes.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:25.443
I have.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:29.600
- [Interviewer] Is it
the father of your child?

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:30.433
- No.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.053
Childhood love.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.510
Still know him.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:40.853
Still in love with him.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:44.300
I first met him when I was a kid.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:46.103
Brings me joy, happiness.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:48.380
It's not easy, but...

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:52.388
- [Interviewer] How old are you now?

00:07:52.388 --> 00:07:54.057
- A little old.

00:07:54.057 --> 00:07:56.022
- [Interviewer] You don't look old.

00:07:56.022 --> 00:07:57.477
- I'm a little old.

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:59.123
I'm in my thirties.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.627
- [Interviewer] How do you
think this life has changed you?

00:08:03.733 --> 00:08:05.313
- Help me understand more.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:08.883
It helped me want more.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:11.870
It opened my eyes.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.183
I'm kinda glad I experienced it.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.410
I've been through it, going through it.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:22.023
'Cause, I don't know.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.850
Sometimes you need to go to struggle

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:30.363
and to see what you really got out there.

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.180
I mean, some people
may say I sound stupid,

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.930
but everybody wasn't handed a spoon,

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:38.110
and got money and family
do this and do that.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:39.690
My life was hard.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:40.763
It still is hard.

00:08:43.892 --> 00:08:47.620
But, knowing now that
it's a different way out.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:49.500
I knew it was a different
way out as a kid,

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.570
but I didn't know.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:51.653
'Cause I was a kid.

00:08:54.812 --> 00:08:56.312
But, I don't regret it though.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:58.380
Do I regret being prostitute?

00:08:58.380 --> 00:08:59.380
No, I don't.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.150
Do I regret anything I've done?

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.963
Maybe a few things, but no, not really.

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:09.720
Because it's going to make me to be

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.463
a strong individual that I am.

00:09:15.535 --> 00:09:17.331
- [Interviewer] Did you have
dreams of a different life

00:09:17.331 --> 00:09:18.681
when you were a young girl?

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:20.630
What'd you want to do?

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.730
- Be a housewife with a job,

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:24.593
and be a mother.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.783
But, I wanted to be a
cook and a beautician.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:33.620
That's me doing hair and cooking.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.887
And have two, maybe three kids.

00:09:36.887 --> 00:09:38.410
And a husband.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.630
Be there at home, cooking, cleaning,

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:41.550
taking care of the kids.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.190
Make sure my husband got what he need.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.023
He satisfied.

00:09:44.023 --> 00:09:45.510
He's not wanting for nothing.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.300
But still, I'm going to
get up and go to work.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.890
Cause I need to make my money,

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:50.723
match his.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.173
- [Interviewer] They can all still happen.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:56.970
You're still young.

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:04.870
What's the worst thing
that's happened to you

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:05.770
in this lifestyle?

00:10:11.355 --> 00:10:13.240
- Losing my son.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:14.393
- [Interviewer] Losing your son?

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.953
Is it because you're working
the street that, that happened?

00:10:23.609 --> 00:10:24.442
- Yup.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:32.210
I mean, I refuse to go,

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:34.360
or hear my son ever say

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:35.670
he don't have.

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.400
I can't do for him.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.140
'Cause I can do anything.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:40.910
I don't care what it'll take.

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:45.910
I'll do anything to make
sure my son is not without.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:48.583
Ever.

00:10:49.605 --> 00:10:51.323
I don't care what nobody says.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.370
Nobody, and I do have a lot
of pride with asking people

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:56.660
for handouts and this.

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.440
I'm gonna get it on my own.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.250
That's my child.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:02.850
That's mine, he belongs to me.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.700
So therefore, it's my responsibility.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.545
Because I can ask somebody for something,

00:11:08.545 --> 00:11:10.820
but well, they have a choice.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:13.830
I don't, because he's mine.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.190
So I'll do whatever it takes.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.600
And if I have to do it today again

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.240
for my child, I'll do whatever.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:20.073
Yes.

00:11:24.201 --> 00:11:25.034
- [Interviewer] All right time.

00:11:25.034 --> 00:11:26.462
Thank you so much for sharing your story.

00:11:26.462 --> 00:11:27.412
- Thank you.

00:11:27.412 --> 00:11:29.579
- [Interviewer] Thank you.

